ANNOUNCE -- a recorded message as a ROM of CVSD bits: no codec on the card.
  message 4.0 s + 0.25 s of silence each end = 288000 bits = 36000 bytes (crc32 020b1b95); 8 kB a second, so 8 s fills a 64 kB part.
  played back through the decoder: segSNR 16.96 dB against the recording -- the codec's own figure, since the bits ARE the codec's.
  first and last 32 bits of the image: 10101010101010101010101010101010 ... 10101010101010101010101010101010

  The seams.  The port sends FILL (1010) until it answers, then the image, then FILL again; a looping
  announcement joins the image's end to its start.  FILL free-runs, so the image can start on either
  phase of it.  Peak within 20 ms of the seam, dB re speech rms (-99.9 = nothing):
  seam                                      in phase  a bit doubled
  idle -> image (the card answers)             -99.9          -30.5
  image -> idle (the message ends)             -99.9          -30.5
  image -> image (a loop)                      -99.9          -30.5
  the image is 288000 bits, an even number, so a start in phase is an end in phase and a loop in phase:
  one gate that starts the address counter on the right half of FILL makes all three seams exact.

  An image cut out of running speech (no silence recorded round it): the decoder starts cold, with its
  integrator and its syllabic filter at idle while the recording's were not.
