  "No claim" is the safe failure: the origin times out and returns cannot-complete.  In the rows where a
  monitor must have seen the rise, one monitor in five was busy at the seizure; with the origin guard, a caller
  whose next digit began within 258 ms of the last is abandoned too.  140 ms is the shortest pause ANY pulse
  register can read (register.md 3.2): below it two digits run together, flood or no flood.

  A CARRIER THAT IS NOT A PAIR.  exch-network.md 1.1 allows a carrier "about 2 ms of A path
  a hop".  A copper pair and an MF8 link are inside it; a leased circuit, a radio, a mux or a
  jitter buffer are tens of milliseconds.  The question rule 2 has to answer is whether that
  eats the origin's guard.  Both rules on, a pause of 140-900 ms, 128 ms guard, 1000 calls a row.

   carrier a hop  the flood is up at |   right %  no claim %  FALSE
            0 ms               46 ms |      73.3        26.7      0
            5 ms               80 ms |      69.1        30.9      0
           10 ms              116 ms |      70.8        29.2      0
           20 ms              186 ms |      72.1        27.9      0
           40 ms              326 ms |      70.5        29.5      0
           80 ms              606 ms |      72.2        27.8      0
          160 ms             1166 ms |      72.8        27.2      0

  IT DOES NOT.  A constant delay moves the whole of the caller's hook -- the seizure that
  opens the flood AND the pulses that follow it -- by the same amount, because they are the
  same wire.  An exchange seven hops out is patched in 600 ms later in absolute time and
  still sees the rise before the first break of `e`, because the break is 600 ms later too.
  **The two delays cancel exactly, and the guard never sees the carrier at all.**

  What DOES eat the guard is delay the carrier LOSES between the seizure and the next
  digit -- a buffer draining, a link re-timing -- because that moves the pulses forward
  against a rise that has already gone by.  A millisecond of it costs a millisecond of
  guard, exactly as the marker's own forwarding does.  Per hop, on top of the marker:

    wander a hop   spread |          guard 128 ms          |       guard matched to the spread       
                   over 7 |   right %  no claim %  FALSE |    tap  a dial of 140-900        of 720-900
                          |                              |         FALSE   abandoned  FALSE  abandoned
