MAINBOARD -- what a ribbon costs the fabric.  Eight blocks a quarter metre apart on one cable;
  5.1 ns/m, 105 ohm, 49 pF/m.  The same 400000 draws as `timing`, with the clock's own flight added.

  1. The clock travels down the same cable as the data, so most of the flight cancels.
     Data reaches listener b at  dly(a) + flight(a,b); b's strobe at  dly(b).  The loss is the difference,
     and it is ZERO whenever the driver is nearer the clock card than the listener.
       clock card at one end       worst loss  17.9 ns   (block 7 driving, block 0 listening)
       clock card in the middle    worst loss   8.9 ns   (block 7 driving, block 3 listening)
       clock card at the far end   worst loss  17.9 ns   (block 0 driving, block 7 listening)
     Hold is never worse than with no cable at all: the same term that shortens set-up lengthens hold.

  2. The worst pair on the worst placement (18 ns), against `timing` 4's figures:
       set-up at the capture flip-flop    725 ns   (no cable: 743)
       hold                               933 ns   (no cable: 915)
     The data's own flight is already inside the 12 ns `wire` term of §4; the clock's was not.

  3. The §3.2 hazard is untouched.  HCLK and SLOTQ leave the clock card together and arrive together:
     their skew is conductor to conductor in one ribbon, under 0.1 ns/m, not the 5 ns/m of the cable.
       address settled    367 ns before the enable, as it was

  4. The line itself, and why the cure for an edge is to slow it down.
     2 m, 8 taps: 193 pF, one way 10.2 ns, round trip 20.4 ns.  A '244 alone gives a 17 ns edge,
          33 ohm in series: edge   31 ns,  1.6 % of a cell   <- slower than the round trip: no ringing to settle
         100 ohm in series: edge   59 ns,  3.0 % of a cell   <- slower than the round trip: no ringing to settle
         220 ohm in series: edge  110 ns,  5.7 % of a cell   <- slower than the round trip: no ringing to settle
         a clock line draws C.V.f = 0.49 mA at 512 kHz, 0.99 mA at 1.024 MHz
     4 m, 8 taps: 290 pF, one way 20.4 ns, round trip 40.8 ns.  A '244 alone gives a 26 ns edge,
          33 ohm in series: edge   47 ns,  2.4 % of a cell   <- slower than the round trip: no ringing to settle
         100 ohm in series: edge   89 ns,  4.6 % of a cell   <- slower than the round trip: no ringing to settle
         220 ohm in series: edge  166 ns,  8.5 % of a cell   <- slower than the round trip: no ringing to settle
         a clock line draws C.V.f = 0.74 mA at 512 kHz, 1.49 mA at 1.024 MHz

  Nothing here is near a limit.  The cure for the edges is to SLOW them: the budget is three quarters
  of a microsecond and the line is ten nanoseconds long, so a series resistor spends what it likes.

  [mainboard: 0 s]

CONFER -- a conference bridge with no codec in it.  CVSD bits cannot be added, but they can be switched:
  each leg watches its caller's bits for speech, one leg holds the floor, and every other leg sends its
  caller the floor-holder's bits as they stand; the holder's own caller hears FILL.
  Speech detector: runs of three equal bits counted over a 16 ms window (1024 bits); "spoke" = the count
  reached a threshold.  No decoder, no RC: a shift register, two gates, a counter.

  1. Windows reaching the threshold, % of all 16 ms windows.  Speech: talkers 1-4, 12 s each with
     their own pauses, plus room noise 45 dB below nominal speech; for speech the % of windows in which the
     talker's own 16 ms rms is within 30 dB of nominal is given alongside, as what "talking" means.
     source                                   4 or more   8 or more  16 or more  32 or more   talking
     speech +0 dB re nominal                       69.0        66.0        60.4        50.2      73.5
     speech -10 dB re nominal                      52.7        46.5        37.8        23.5      64.1
     speech -20 dB re nominal                      27.9        19.8        10.0         2.5      43.9
     speech -30 dB re nominal                       4.3         1.4         0.4         0.0      21.7
     room noise alone, -20 dB                    100.00      100.00       99.97       16.70
     room noise alone, -30 dB                     34.43        0.10        0.00        0.00
     room noise alone, -40 dB                      0.00        0.00        0.00        0.00
     FILL (an idle leg)                            0.00        0.00        0.00        0.00
     -> threshold 8: a counter's Q3.  Room noise 30 dB down reaches it once in a thousand windows, 40 dB
        down never; speech 20 dB down reaches it in a window in five, which the hangover (2) holds between.

  2. Three callers, 14 s, room noise 45 dB down in every microphone.  A speaks 0.5-4.5 s; B says "mm-hm"
     at 2.6-3.0 s while A talks, then answers at 4.6 s, 100 ms after A stops, until 8.5 s; C speaks
     9.0-13.0 s, and A talks over C from 10.5 to 12.0 s.  Legs in slot order A, B, C.

     "switched" is the change of floor against the word's onset, the first sample within 20 dB of nominal:
     negative is before it.
