TRAFFIC -- demand that is not smooth, and the same network at peak and off peak.
  Every line is a SOURCE here, not a Poisson stream: it hangs up, waits, and lifts again,
  so the offered load falls as the exchange fills.  That is what `make traffic-net` has in the
  gates -- a line is a state machine there and there is no other kind of caller -- and this
  runs on the same clock as the RTL bench: the dial and the register's three windows eight
  times life, the marker's 8 ms scan and its 7 ms hop untouched.

  The network: 8 exchanges, two hubs on one link with three leaves each, six channels
  every link, 12 lines an exchange, 96 in all.  Three registers, three monitor cells a
  block, three resolvers.  30 % of calls leave home; a conversation averages 0.5 s.
  Phase 1 is 10 s, measured after 6 s of warm-up -- a phase begins with every
  line on hook, which is not a state the network is ever in otherwise, and the warm-up is
  twice the longer idle so that what is measured is a network in its stride.  Off peak a line
  waits 3000 ms between calls and at peak 1500 ms.  `make traffic-net` runs the same two
  phases in the gates.  2 to 4 are not tied to that and run 240 s, because a 1 % loss is
  not a measurement over a few hundred seizures.

