  6 tandems fully connected, 6 ch           6 exchanges -- more than four directions
     Wherever there is anything BEHIND a hub, the mesh spends directions and trunk groups
     and moves no traffic worth the name: the only calls a strapped tandem-to-tandem link
     can carry are the twelve lines at one end calling the twelve at the other.  Capacity
     between hubs is bought in CHANNELS on the tree, not in links off it.
     The last pair is the exception that shows why.  With NOTHING behind them, the hubs
     ARE the traffic, every pair has a direct link and no call is ever forwarded -- and
     the mesh is better than the chain by a factor of five.  Five is also where it stops:
     a sixth exchange would want a fifth direction, and any exchange hung off one of the
     five turns every one of those links back into a strap that nothing may cross.

  5. What actually reaches sixty-four codes: SMALLER EXCHANGES.  Both the traffic and the
     flood on every link are proportional to the LINES in the network and not to the
     exchanges, so the way to sixty-four is exchanges that are not full.
